   The array becomes [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 3] after sorting the first five elements.
   The array becomes [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] after sorting all elements.

The time complexity of the modified Insertion Sort is O(n^2), where n is the number of elements in the array. This is because we have two nested loops: one to iterate through each element of the array and the other to compare and move the elements in the sorted subarray.

The space complexity of the modified Insertion Sort is O(1) because it does not require additional space proportional to the input size. It performs the sorting in-place, modifying the original array.

Now, let's compare the modified Insertion Sort with Quick Sort and Merge Sort:

1. Quick Sort:
   - Time Complexity: The average case time complexity of Quick Sort is O(n log n), which is faster than the modified Insertion Sort's O(n^2). However, the worst-case time complexity of Quick Sort is O(n^2), which can occur when the pivot chosen is the smallest or largest element in the array.
   - Space Complexity: Quick Sort has a space complexity of O(log n) on average due to the recursive calls, but it can go up to O(n) in the worst-case scenario.
   - Use Cases: Quick Sort is generally preferred for large datasets or when a fast sorting algorithm is required. It is commonly used in many programming languages' standard library sorting functions.

2. Merge Sort:
   - Time Complexity: Merge Sort has a time complexity of O(n log n) in all cases, which is better than the modified Insertion Sort's O(n^2). However, Merge Sort requires additional space proportional to the input size, making it less memory-efficient compared to the modified Insertion Sort.
   - Space Complexity: Merge Sort has a space complexity of O(n) since it requires an additional array to merge the sorted subarrays.
   - Use Cases: Merge Sort is suitable for large datasets where stability and a consistent worst-case time complexity are important factors. It is often used in external sorting when the entire data cannot fit into memory.

Advantages of Modified Insertion Sort:
- Simplicity: The modified Insertion Sort algorithm is straightforward to understand and implement.
- Space Efficiency: The modified Insertion Sort performs sorting in-place, using minimal additional space.
- Performance for Small Datasets: When the input size is small or nearly sorted, the modified Insertion Sort can outperform more complex algorithms due to its simplicity and lower overhead.

Disadvantages of Modified Insertion Sort:
- Higher Time Complexity: The modified Insertion Sort has a time complexity of O(n^2), making it less efficient compared to algorithms like Quick Sort or Merge Sort for larger datasets.
- Sensitivity to Initial Order: The modified Insertion Sort's performance heavily depends on the initial order of the elements. It can be slower when the input is almost sorted in descending order.

