Write a Java program that takes in a string and reverses the order of its characters, while also removing any duplicate characters. The program should ignore any punctuation or special characters and should only consider alphanumeric characters. The program should also keep track of the frequency of each character and display the characters in descending order of frequency.

Additionally, implement the program using a custom data structure that allows efficient retrieval of both the frequency and the order of appearance for each character. The time complexity for adding a character to the data structure should be O(1), and the time complexity for retrieving the characters in descending order of frequency should be O(n log n), where n is the length of the string.

Furthermore, ensure that the program can handle input strings of up to 1 million characters in length efficiently, without exceeding a memory usage of 10 MB.

### Response:
import java.util.*;

class CharacterFrequency {
    private char character;
    private int frequency;
    private int index;

    public CharacterFrequency(char character, int frequency, int index) {
        this.character = character;
        this.frequency = frequency;
        this.index = index;
    }

    public char getCharacter() {
        return character;
    }

    public int getFrequency() {
        return frequency;
    }

    public int getIndex() {
        return index;
    }
}

class CharacterFrequencyComparator implements Comparator<CharacterFrequency> {
    @Override
    public int compare(CharacterFrequency cf1, CharacterFrequency cf2) {
        if (cf1.getFrequency() != cf2.getFrequency()) {
            return cf2.getFrequency() - cf1.getFrequency();
        } else {
            return cf1.getIndex() - cf2.getIndex();
        }
    }
}

class CharacterTracker {
    private Map<Character, CharacterFrequency> characterMap;
    private PriorityQueue<CharacterFrequency> frequencyQueue;
    private int index;

    public CharacterTracker() {
        characterMap = new HashMap<>();
        frequencyQueue = new PriorityQueue<>(new CharacterFrequencyComparator());
        index = 0;
    }

    public void addCharacter(char character) {
        if (characterMap.containsKey(character)) {
            CharacterFrequency cf = characterMap.get(character);
            cf.frequency++;
            characterMap.put(character, cf);
        } else {
            CharacterFrequency cf = new CharacterFrequency(character, 1, index++);
            characterMap.put(character, cf);
            frequencyQueue.offer(cf);
        }
    }

    public List<CharacterFrequency> getCharacterFrequencies() {
        List<CharacterFrequency> characterFrequencies = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!frequencyQueue.isEmpty()) {
            characterFrequencies.add(frequencyQueue.poll());
        }
        return characterFrequencies;
    }
}

public class CharacterReverse {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a string: ");
        String input = scanner.nextLine();

        CharacterTracker characterTracker = new CharacterTracker();
        for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
            if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(c)) {
                characterTracker.addCharacter(c);
            }
        }

        List<CharacterFrequency> characterFrequencies = characterTracker.getCharacterFrequencies();
        StringBuilder reversedString = new StringBuilder();
        for (CharacterFrequency cf : characterFrequencies) {
            reversedString.append(cf.getCharacter());
        }

        System.out.println("Reversed string with no duplicate characters: " + reversedString.toString());
    }
}

