Write a Java program for sorting a given list of numbers in descending order without using any built-in sorting functions or libraries. You are only allowed to use the bubble sort algorithm. The time complexity of your algorithm should be O(n^2) and the space complexity should be O(1).

Additionally, you are not allowed to use any temporary variables for swapping elements in the list. Instead, you must use bitwise operations to perform the swaps.

unsortedList = [7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]

### Response:
Here is a Java program that sorts a given list of numbers in descending order using the bubble sort algorithm and bitwise operations for swapping elements:

```java
public class BubbleSortDescending {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] unsortedList = {7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1};

        bubbleSortDescending(unsortedList);

        System.out.println("Sorted list in descending order:");
        for (int num : unsortedList) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }

    public static void bubbleSortDescending(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        boolean swapped;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            swapped = false;

            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (arr[j] < arr[j + 1]) {
                    // Swap the elements using bitwise XOR operation
                    arr[j] = arr[j] ^ arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = arr[j] ^ arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j] = arr[j] ^ arr[j + 1];
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }

            // If no swaps occurred in the inner loop, the array is already sorted
            if (!swapped) {
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
